crowned by a sweeping dish and pumping a piston needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already
on your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and
the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json`
— together with the rig you define are your submission.

Voxels are **opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency — placed as
discrete cells with `set-voxel`, `fill-box`, `stroke-box`, 3D lines, spheres, and a
mirror plane. Every voxel operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each
part is sculpted **separately** in its own preview and log. The volume starts
**empty**. Each call only records the operation to that part's log and renders
**nothing** — run `voxel-anim render` to (re)draw the previews and the assembled
scene (`--component <part>` redraws one part; `--time <ms> --animation <name>`
renders the model posed at an instant of an animation), and you **must** run
`voxel-anim render` before you finish so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry the
run is scored from — an unrendered part is scored as empty.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each
a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the
required animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation
targets a part with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it; each
part is sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates. The field starts **empty**.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name works
voxel-anim fill-box --part works --x0 1 --y0 0 --z0 1 --x1 5 --y1 5 --z1 5 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig and animation subcommands
(defining parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring animations with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`parts/<part>.png` after you run `voxel-anim render`** to see that part's progress, **and read the
assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`,
`scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole foundry composed from all your parts,
drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene views are how you confirm the parts
actually fit together: that the dish sits centered on its mast at the crown and the
piston seats on its flank. Because each part is sculpted in the shared volume's
coordinates, position each part where it belongs on the finished works; the scene
simply composes them there.

